.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped \- declare, initialise, and auto-destroy a ccol_event_loop on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped(name, max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                            num_reactor_threads);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a ccol_event_loop variable with the name name. It
tags that variable with the cleanup attribute of GCC and Clang. It
then calls ccol_event_loop_create() to initialise the variable. It
calls ccol_fatal_err() when the create fails. The library destroys the
variable automatically when it leaves its scope, with no explicit
ccol_event_loop_destroy() call. That destroy shuts every reactor
thread down, joins each one, and frees every registration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                  Name of the variable for the ccol_event_loop handle.
max_events_per_wait   Size of the event batch buffer of the polling
                      thread. The value must be from 1 to INT_MAX. It
                      must also be small enough that max_events_per_wait
                      times the size of one kernel event record (struct
                      epoll_event on Linux, struct kevent on FreeBSD)
                      does not overflow size_t.
num_lock_stripes      Number of independent lock stripes for the registry
                      of fds and entries. The value must be >= 1. A
                      value of 1 means one shared lock.
num_reactor_threads   Number of background reactor threads. The value
                      must be >= 1. A value of 1 means one thread that
                      polls and dispatches in line.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro calls ccol_fatal_err() on a failure. A caller may rely on
no callback being able to reach anything in the scope of this loop.
One such thing is a mutex, or a buffer that the arg of a callback
points at. That reliance must hold up to the join at the exit of the
scope, and it must include that join. It is not enough for it to hold
up to an explicit ccol_event_loop_remove(3) call before that point.
See the notes of ccol_event_loop_remove(3) about a callback that
outlives the return of that call. The implicit shutdown of this scope
has the same property.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped(loop, 32, 1, 1);
    ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                                    handlers, NULL, NULL);
    /* ... */
}  /* the library shuts loop down here, joins every reactor thread,
      and destroys the loop */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_construct (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
